 "caveats": "Only stocks still listed today (delisted and acquired companies are missing). Snapshots every 10 sessions overlap, so these are averages, not independent trades. Averages are trimmed at the 0.5% tails. Past results are evidence, not a promise.",
 "rules": "Stage 2 = close above the 150-day (30-week) average, which rose more than 1% in 20 sessions — the same as the Stage column elsewhere on the site. The advance starts at the first Stage 2 day after the last confirmed Stage 4 (10 of 20 sessions below a falling 150-day line). Phase points: base count since the start 0–4 (O'Neil: a base counts after a 20%+ gain from the last counted pivot; undercutting the previous base's low restarts at 1; two bases = 1 point, three = 2, four = 3, five+ = 4) · months in Stage 2 0–2 (6–12 months 1, 12+ months 2) · gain from the low before the start 0–2 (150–300% 1, over 300% 2) · 150-day slope slowing 0–1. Early 0–1, Mid 2–4, Late 5+. Stage 2 Score: RS 25 · 150-day slope 20 · close to the advance's peak 20 · above the 150-day 15 (30–100% best) · above the 50-day 10 · highest close in 6 years 10.",
 "findings": [
  "Late Stage 2 means more risk in both halves of the 20 years: late-phase stocks fell into Stage 4 within 6 months more often (25% vs 19% in 2007–16, 29% vs 21% in 2017–26) and their median worst drop over the next 6 months was about −14% against −10% for early-phase stocks.",
  "Unlike NSE, late did not mean lower returns in the US: late-phase snapshots beat the S&P 500 by +1.9% a year on average against +0.3% for early ones (+1.4% vs −1.2% in 2017–26). Read Late as 'tighten stops and size down', not 'sell'.",
  "The first leg was the best part of the advance: +3.8% a year for stocks with no completed base yet, against about −0.1% to +2% after one or more bases. Stocks with 4+ bases fell into Stage 4 more often (27% against 21%).",
  "The edge faded with time: snapshots 18–24 months into Stage 2 averaged −0.7% a year and 26% fell into Stage 4, against +1–2% in the first year.",
  "Distance above the 150-day line was not a warning: stocks 65–100% above it beat the index by +16.5% a year against −0.5% for those within 5% of it, and fewer fell into Stage 4 (10% vs 36%). More than 100% above the 150-day line averaged +26% but with a median worst 6-month drop of about −30%, so it is shown as a climax warning rather than counted as lateness.",
  "The Stage 2 Score sorted outcomes in both halves: from about −0.6% a year under 40 to +6.7% at 85+, with falls into Stage 4 dropping from 31% to 11% (2007–16: +2.2% → +5.2% for 70–84; 2017–26: −3.1% → +8.5% for 85+). Best combinations: Mid or Early with a score of 70+ (+7.1% and +6.6% a year, 11–12% fell into Stage 4). Worst: Early with a score under 55 (−1.3%) and Late under 55 (36% fell into Stage 4 within 6 months)."
 ]
